// window/x11/bind/xinput2.cst — XInput2: input the core protocol cannot express.
//
// Core X gives one pointer and one keyboard, positions as integers, and no way
// to distinguish two mice. XI2 replaces all of it: devices are hierarchical
// (master and slave), coordinates are fixed-point with sub-pixel precision, and
// a tablet's pressure and tilt arrive as valuators alongside the position.
//
// The two this layer wants:
//
//   XISelectEvents with XI_RawMotion
//     Raw motion is the pointer's movement BEFORE the server applies
//     acceleration and before it clamps at the screen edge. That is what makes
//     cursor_mode(RELATIVE) real — a first-person camera driven by cooked
//     motion stops turning when the pointer reaches the edge of the screen,
//     and warping the pointer back to the centre to work around it produces
//     its own motion events.
//
//   XIQueryVersion
//     The negotiation. XI2 events do not arrive until a client asks for a
//     version the server also speaks.
//
// XI2 events arrive as GenericEvents whose payload lives in libX11's own memory
// until XFreeEventData. Anything that stores one has to copy it first, which is
// why ../window.cst's pump copies the payload before the ring rather than after.

extern "libXi.so.6" fn XIAllowEvents(display as *u8, deviceid as i32, event_mode as i32,
                                     time as i64) as i32;
extern "libXi.so.6" fn XIAllowTouchEvents(display as *u8, deviceid as i32, touchid as u32,
                                          grab_window as i64, event_mode as i32) as i32;
